            <ArticleTitle>Jejunojejunum intussusception as the single initial manifestation of Henoch-Schönlein purpura in a teenager.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2085.e1-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ajem.2011.12.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0735-6757(11)00567-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We described a 14-year-old boy with initial presentation of recurrent intermittent abdominal pain, with subsequent development of jejunojejunum intussusception. The characteristic purpuric skin rash of Henoch-Schönlein purpura appeared later after the surgical manual reduction. When abdominal pain is the single initial manifestation in patients with undiagnosed Henoch-Schönlein purpura, emergency physicians should evaluate if the patient required early surgical intervention such as intussusception or perforation and always keep Henoch-Schönlein purpura as a possibility in mind.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
